Answer: According to the search result, Comet Ping Pong owner James Alefantis is "a big Democratic Party supporter and raised money for both Barack Obama and Hillary Clinton," and he appeared in hacked campaign emails "in connection with the fundraisers." However, the source notes that Alefantis has "never met Clinton," and it does not document any personal or financial relationships between the restaurant's employees and Hillary Clinton, Bill Clinton, or the Democratic National Committee.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/blogs-trending-38156985

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, here is the step-by-step analysis and conclusion of the fact-check:

### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence thoroughly debunks the "Pizzagate" conspiracy theory, which alleges that prominent Democrats, including Hillary and Bill Clinton, operated a child sex-trafficking ring out of a Washington, D.C. pizzeria. Investigations by law enforcement and even an armed individual who entered the restaurant, Comet Ping Pong, to "self-investigate" confirmed that the establishment does not even have a basement, let alone any underground facilities or evidence of child trafficking. Furthermore, there are no police reports, court cases, or credible physical or digital evidence supporting these claims, which originated from online message boards misinterpreting leaked emails. No Democratic Party members have been arrested or indicted in connection with such allegations, and the restaurant's owner has never even met Hillary Clinton. Because the evidence is exceptionally clear and comprehensive, no further investigation is required.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the overwhelming evidence from law enforcement investigations, the physical reality of the location, and the documented origins of the conspiracy theory, the claim is completely false. The pizzeria in question does not possess a basement, and multiple investigations have found absolutely no evidence of any child sex-trafficking ring or involvement by Hillary and Bill Clinton. Therefore, the final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The allegation is completely refuted as multiple investigations have confirmed that the Washington, D.C. pizzeria, Comet Ping Pong, does not even have a basement or any underground facilities ([The Cut](https://www.thecut.com/2016/12/to-understand-pizzagate-it-helps-to-understand-cults.html)). Law enforcement agencies dismissed the claims as a "fake news conspiracy theory" ([NBC Washington](https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/local/man-with-assault-rifle-arrested-at-comet-ping-pong-in-nw-dc/111295/)), and there is absolutely no credible physical, digital, or forensic evidence linking Hillary or Bill Clinton to the restaurant ([Christian Science Monitor](https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/2016/1205/Gunman-at-Comet-Ping-Pong-sought-to-self-investigate-fake-Clinton-report)). The baseless rumor, known as "Pizzagate," originated from online message boards misinterpreting leaked emails ([Billboard](https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/teigen-legend-pizzagate-conspiracy-timeline-8085816/)). No victims or witnesses have filed complaints, and an armed man who entered the pizzeria to "self-investigate" admitted he found no evidence of harm ([PMQ](https://www.pmq.com/the-lessons-of-pizzagate/)). Furthermore, no Democratic Party members have been arrested or indicted ([USA Today](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/06/18/fact-check-ellen-degeneres-oprah-winfrey-others-not-house-arrest/5333585002/)), and the restaurant's owner has never even met Hillary Clinton ([BBC](https://www.bbc.com/news/blogs-trending-38156985)).